
This file contains changes and release history of the O.C.T. system.
Changes are added at the top of this file.

-brian


------------------------------------------------------------------
19 SEPTEMEBER 1993:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.99.x)

  Translator:
  	Now handles instance variables "unsigned foo;" correctly.  Before,
	the `unsigned' by itself would terminate the decoding string.

	Option "-Wall" is equivalent to "-w2".  For compatibility with
	the GNU and NeXT compilers.

  CPP:
  	This version of the preprocessor has now been compiled with Borland
	C++ version 3.1 (even though you won't need it).

  Class Library Source:
  	Eliminated all (?) of the #import directives from the header files
	and the library source code.

------------------------------------------------------------------
xx JULY 1992:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.95.0)

  Translator:
    The translator is growing slowly.  The runtime string space is
	increasing.  Piping the output of 'oct' won't place "Phase 2"
	messages in your output anymore.  It doesn't seem to work with
	Manx C 3.4A ... :-(

------------------------------------------------------------------
02 JULY 1992:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.94.3)

  License:
    A few people have asked by licensing.  I now have the correct lingo.
	All the source files (I hope) have been updated to reflect this
	clarification.

    The translator is FREEWARE.  The translator and the support tools are
	Copyright (C) 1989, 1990, 1991, 19992 by Brian J. Witt, ALL RIGHTS
	RESERVED.  It may be freely distributed for non-commericial use only.
	Anything else requires written permission from the author(s).
	There is no warrenty and no guarentee of fitness.
    This includes 'oct', 'prelink' and 'mkif'.   Not covered is the
	DECUS 'cpp'. If you make modifications and/or improvements, I ask
	that your changes be foled into the software tools.

    The runtime system and its support files are in the public domain.
	The runtime object and source code may be freely copied without
	restriction.  There is no warrenty and no guarentee of fitness.
	You may do with them as you see fit.  Any changes, however, should
	be marked as not being included in the original source code.

  Translator:
    OCT deals with "{id foo; short xx[4];}"  doesn't error any more.  For
	instance variables, you truely get "@sssss" in the string description
	in the instance structure.  A sub-structure now is surrounded by
	"{ ... }".  So "{id foo; struct node *node;}" will become "@{^}",
	which is close to what it should really be.

  Runtime:
    The file subio.c has been updated to just print the curly braces
	when they appear in a print template string.

  Makefiles:
    The Makefiles have been cleaned up a little bit.


------------------------------------------------------------------
19 APR 1992:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.15):

  include/objc/private/config.h
    The list of environment variables to check to find the path of
	the temp directory is now a file list.  These is a 'catch' at
	the bottom of the file in case EV_TEMPDIR is left undefined.
	Help will print all variables that will be checked.

  Makefile
    The Makefile variable 'LFLAGS' was used for linking programs.
	But, it is supposed to be used for lex rules.  Now the variable
	'LDFLAGS' is used (the better way).

------------------------------------------------------------------
19 APR 1992:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.14):

  OCT Grammar:
    Moving towards the Objective-C grammar under NeXT version 2, the
    @abstract directive is now @protocol.

------------------------------------------------------------------
09 AUG 1991:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.13B):

  Command Lines :
    There are Makefiles for ATT&T UNIX and SunOS4 included.  Manx Amiga
	Makefiles are always there :-).

	The -I option to specify include directories has the space after
	it removed in the Makefiles.  However, OCT will  read the next arg
	if there is nothing after the "-I", it reads the next arg.
	Unfortuneately, not all C compilers are this nice...

  Runtime Substrate:
    A file may now have a class definition and main() inside of it.
	Before, the startup code was acknowledging the class.  Thanks to
	Dan Banay for this bug report!
	
	Dan also pointed out how to add variable argument lists.  The switch
	USE_STDARG in oct/include/octhead.h controls this.  __STDC__ turns
	it on automagically.  USE_STDARG assumes an ANSI-C <stdarg.h> file
	and vprintf() and cousins exist.

    Printing objects (via "oc_show") isn't the greeatest techniquie
	in the world.  Feel free to hack.

------------------------------------------------------------------
12 MAY 1991:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.12):

  TRANSLATOR :
  	Now, you must place all functions after the @implementation clause
	for class defining files, or after @includes() for non-class defining
	files.  This way, the objC runtime includes are available.

	When running cpp, the command line arg "-+" is added for you (see file
	"xlator/config.h" for details).

	If touuper('5') != '5', then define SW_NEED_2LO_2UP is config.h header.
	This is used by Dynix users.

------------------------------------------------------------------
10 MAY 1991:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.11):

  class: BYTEARRAY:
  	The string copmare now correctly searches for a whole matching
	string, instead of just a few chars at the beginning.


------------------------------------------------------------------
29 APRIL 1991:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.10):

  TRANSLATOR:
    If a structure element is the same name as a typedef, it is OK.


------------------------------------------------------------------
5 APRIL 1991:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.93.7):
    The UNIX version is now better working.  Makefile's still have
	to be manually converted, though.  Anyone like the idea of
	having "makefile.cpp" files?

	Along with some beta-testers, the dependence on the NULL ptr
    holding a NULL ptr is being removed. "the sparc is lint in
	hardware" -- db.

  TRANSLATOR:
    When parsing "compare( [obj value], [there value] )" the comma
	in the middle would not be output.  The comma now appears!
	Also, better processing of exprs inside of for() and if()
	statements, and the expr that follows!

	The @public directive is now implemented.  This means a class
	may reference instance vars defined by its superclasses if
	they want them accessed.  Be carefull here, since these superclass
	accesses will hardcode offsets into the code.

  FOUNDATION CLASSES:
    There is now a linked-list class and a List class.  The List class
	is modeled after List on the NeXT.

------------------------------------------------------------------
15 NOV 1990:
  DISTRIBUTION (1.92.6):
  	More files have been placed in the file "whole.zoo".  Amiga specific
	files have been placed in "amibin.zoo".  This includes executable
	version of all programs that the user can build themselves.
	Also, macro files for Manx's sdb program are included here.
	This should make moving the system to a non-Amiga base easier.

  TRANSLATOR:
  	The grammar trace option is now -zt.  Affects only 'oct' hackers.


------------------------------------------------------------------
11 NOV 1990:
  CLASS SYSTEM:
  	The new class system handles misc system interfacing stuff.
	This is my own extension, based loosely on the System class in
	Actor by The Whitewater Group (R).  UNTESTED!  Class System can
	beep and inspect/modify (real soon) the .info file for the project
	(application datafile).  On the NeXT this uses NXReadDefault().
	UNTESTED!
	
	The method dumpDictionary: has been moved from Object into System.

  SUBSTRATE:
  	The defines PTR2INT() and OC_HASH_MASK are added for better
	portability.  PTR2INT converts pointers into generic integer
	things, usually a long.  The hash mask is to keep the return
	values the same when treated as a short.  Hash value should
	always be positive.

  CPP:
  	The cpp program has been augmented to trim away the slash-slash
	comment.  (Cmd line option "-+", GNU cpp compatible.)  Compiles are
	now quieter. Comment lines like below don't cause warnings anymore.
		//	This isn't going to mess up.
	
	The cpp program provided here is no longer compatible with the
	standard cpp program, even though the name remains the same... :-(.

------------------------------------------------------------------
23 OCT 1990:

  DISTRIBUTION:
  	The layout of the files in the distribution has been changed:
	the directories are now "flatter" than before.  The source for
	'cpp' is stored in the "xlator/ccpp" directory.

------------------------------------------------------------------
15 OCT 1990:

  CPP:
  	Yes, my version of Decus 'cpp' now accepts #import directives.
	Unmodified source from a NeXT will now be accepted.  The current cpp
	program has stack overflow checking enabled.  A stack of 12000 is very
	nice.  The cpp program will not read any environment variables.

------------------------------------------------------------------
08 OCT 1990:

  TRANSLATOR:	1.92.5
  	For the Amiga, we now search the Manx environment variables, then
	search ENV: for getenv() requests.  This means Lattice people can
	interface with O.C.T.
	The definition for 'errno' is included from <errno.h>.  This makes
	good for Microsoft C :-).

  RUNTIME SUBSTRATE:
  	Many of the structures have been changed to be more NeXT like.
	This means the structures (and field elements) are less StepStone
	like.  Any complaints?

------------------------------------------------------------------
20 SEPT 1990:

  COLLECTION CLASSES:
  	The method [aCollection copy] now copies the collection object
	and the object used to support the collection.  Things in the
	collection are NOT copied (a bug fix, I hope).  The isCopyOf:
	method works in conjuction with this logic.

  AMIGA CLASSES:
  	The ENode and EList classes have been moved to the Amiga-specific
	directory.  Even though the Node structures are included, their
	use is superceded by the List foundation class.

------------------------------------------------------------------
10 SEPT 1990:

  SUBSTRATE:
  	Printing of type RECT and type VECT will now print numbers instead
	of trying to print strings.  Also, the print file (subio.c) is now
	a "C" file instead of being translated.  Boolean variables (BOOL)
	are type "o".  So FALSE prints as "o0".

  TRANSLATOR:	1.92.2
  	Warning 9 (Mismatch in argument type:) will appear if the storage
	class varies for a method definition.  Also, if the parameter
	name varies among a method's definition, warnings appear.  This
	warning is superious, and shouldn't even be noted.  The definition
	used is the first one encountered.  The translator still needs
	some work here..

------------------------------------------------------------------
30 JUNE 1990:

  TRANSLATOR:	1.92.1
  	Private methods are now accepted by the translator without
	generating a warning.  If the method begins with an underscore (_)
	then is doesn't need to be included in the public interface section.


------------------------------------------------------------------
04 MAY 1990:

  TRANSLATOR:	1.92.0
  	The instance variables for your class are always available to
	you.  You reference them by name only, no qualifiers are necessary.
	To reach instance variables of any parent classes, you must
	qualify the reference with "self->".

  CLASSES:
  	After a little review of the classes on the NeXT computer, I've
	noticed that the Collection category of classes has been replaced
	by the single class List.  I follow this closely, except that
	methods for set addition (addIfAbset:) have been pushed down to
	the class Collection.

	Current element count is now "(int) count".

------------------------------------------------------------------

